1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,789
Learning to Observe Behaviour My little pal called our puppy a rascal

2
00:00:04,803 --> 00:00:07,632
because it would gnaw a slipper grab a mitten and make a

3
00:00:07,645 --> 00:00:11,974
mess It might wag its tail at a visitor then growl at

4
00:00:11,987 --> 00:00:17,026
an unfamiliar noise Once a wasp made it leap sideways and howl

5
00:00:17,263 --> 00:00:21,526
a moment later it began to sniff the grass again An animal-care

6
00:00:21,539 --> 00:00:25,421
volunteer asked us not to ascribe every action to a human motive

7
00:00:26,329 --> 00:00:29,592
A hound might follow a scent a cat might twitch a whisker

8
00:00:29,855 --> 00:00:34,355
and a bird might flutter before settling on a perch These behaviours

9
00:00:34,368 --> 00:00:39,658
required observation rather than a story about good or evil intentions We

10
00:00:39,671 --> 00:00:43,895
learnt to avoid teasing the puppy or encouraging it to snap A

11
00:00:43,908 --> 00:00:48,684
youngster should not poke an animal simply to make it react Understanding

12
00:00:48,697 --> 00:00:53,460
grew when we noticed patterns respected space and asked knowledgeable people for

13
00:00:53,474 --> 00:00:58,329
guidance Affection is more useful when it is accompanied by attention to

14
00:00:58,342 --> 00:00:59,763
another creature's needs
